-893344f8f494?random_title=true'], active_page_index=0, last_browser_action=\"\\n\\n\\n\\n\\nfill('1029', '# HyperLogLog Project Specification\\n\\n## Overview\\n\\nThe HyperLogLog (HLL) algorithm is a probabilistic data structure that tracks the cardinality of large data sets. It is particularly useful for scenarios like counting unique users accessing a website where storing each user explicitly would be impractical due to memory constraints.\\n\\n## Key Parameters\\n\\n- **b**: Number of initial bits in a binary representation of a hash value\\n- **m**: Number of registers (buckets) - equal to 2^b\\n- **p**: Leftmost position of 1 (MSBs position of 1)\\n\\n## Algorithm Overview\\n\\n1. Hash the input value and convert to binary representation\\n2. Extract b bits starting from the most significant bit (MSB) to determine the register\\n3. From remaining bits, find the position of the leftmost 1 (number of leading zeros + 1)\\n4. Store max(register[p], current_value) in the register\\n\\n## Cardinality Calculation\\n\\nAfter processing all elements, cardinality is calculated using:\\n\\n```\\nE = constant * m^2 / (sum(2^(-R[j])))\\n```\\n\\nWhere:\\n- constant = 0.79402\\n- R[j] is the value in register j\\n- N = m\\n\\n## Task #1: Basic HyperLogLog Implementation\\n\\nImplement the following functions in `hyperloglog.h`:\\n\\n- `HyperLogLog(initial_bits)`: Constructor with number of leading bits\\n- `GetCardinality()`: Returns cardinality value\\n- `AddElem(val)`: Computes and places value in register\\n- `ComputeCardinality()`: Computes cardinality based on formula\\n\\nHelper functions:\\n- `ComputeBinary(hash_t hash)`: Converts hash to 64-bit binary\\n- `PositionOfLeftmostOne(...)`: Computes position of leftmost 1\\n\\n## Task #2: Presto\\\\'s Dense Layout Implementation\\n\\nImplement in `hyperloglog_presto.h`:\\n\\n- `HyperLogLogPresto(initial_bits)`: Constructor\\n- `AddElem()`: Places value in register\\n- `ComputeCardinality()`: Computes cardinality\\n\\nKey differences:\\n- Counts rightmost contiguous zeros instead of leftmost 1\\n- Uses overflow buckets for large values\\n\\n## Development Setup\\n\\n```bash\\nmkdir build\\ncd build\\ncmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug ..\\nmake -j`nproc`\\n```\\n\\n## Testing\\n\\n```bash\\ncd build\\nmake -j$(nproc) hyperloglog_test\\n./test/hyperloglog_test\\n```\\n\\n## Resources\\n\\n- [HyperLogLog Paper](https://algo.inria.fr/flajolet/Publications/FlFuGaMe07.pdf)\\n- [Meta\\\\'s Presto Implementation](https://engineering.fb.com/2018/12/13/data-infrastructure/hyperloglog/)')\\nnoop(2000)\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\", last_browser_action_error=\"NameError: Invalid action type 'GetCardinality'.\", focused_element_bid='1029'), BrowseInteractiveAction(browser_actions=\"\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\nfill('1029', '# HyperLogLog Project Specification\\n\\n## Overview\\n\\nThe HyperLogLog (HLL) algorithm is a probabilistic data structure that tracks the cardinality of large data sets. It is particularly useful for scenarios like counting unique users accessing a website where storing each user explicitly would be impractical due to memory constraints.\\n\\n## Key Parameters\\n\\n- **b**: Number of initial bits in a binary representation of a hash value\\n- **m**: Number of registers (buckets) - equal to 2^b\\n- **p**: Leftmost position of 1 (MSBs position of 1)\\n\\n## Algorithm Overview\\n\\n1. Hash the input value and convert to binary representation\\n2. Extract b bits starting from the most significant bit (MSB) to determine the register\\n3. From remaining bits, find the position of the leftmost 1 (number of leading zeros + 1)\\n4. Store max(register[p], current_value) in the register\\n\\n## Cardinality Calculation\\n\\nAfter processing all elements, cardinality is calculated using:\\n\\n```\\nE = constant * m^2 / (sum(2^(-R[j])))\\n```\\n\\nWhere:\\n- constant = 0.79402\\n- R[j] is the value in register j\\n- N = m\\n\\n## Task #1: Basic HyperLogLog Implementation\\n\\nImplement the following functions in 